# Invention No. 11 in G minor, BWV 782 (J. S. Bach)
#
# Character: searching and chromatic. The subject is a sixteenth-note
# run (after a sixteenth rest) up from D and back, over a bar and a
# half; against it the other hand has a countersubject that creeps by
# semitones (F-sharp, F, E, E-flat, D down; or E-flat, E, F, F-sharp up).
# The two are invertible and keep changing hands. It wants a thoughtful,
# moderate tempo so the chromatic line is heard, not a toccata.
#
# Time signature: 4/4, 23 bars in the Mutopia score and in the MIDI;
# the bass G is on the downbeat of bar 1 (the top enters after a
# sixteenth rest).
#
# Tempo: quarter = 69, 92 quarters, about 1:23.
# Track times: Angela Hewitt (Hyperion) 1:20, about 69; Gustav
# Leonhardt (harpsichord, Apple Music reissue) 2:00, about 46; Andras
# Schiff (ECM, clavichord) 0:59, about 94; Glenn Gould (Sony, 1964)
# 0:54, about 102. The recordings disagree more than for any other
# invention here. 69 is Hewitt's tempo, in the middle: the falling
# chromatic line keeps its weight and the runs still flow on the chip.
#
# Structure (read from the Mutopia score and checked against the MIDI;
# the key plan is my own reading, no published bar-by-bar analysis was
# used):
#   1-2    Subject, top, G minor; chromatic countersubject falling in
#          the bass (F-sharp F E E-flat D).
#   3-4    Subject, bottom, G minor; the countersubject rising in the
#          top (E-flat E F F-sharp).
#   5      Episode toward D minor.
#   6-7    Subject, bottom, D minor; the top in free counterpoint and a
#          long trilled F.
#   8-9    Episode, suspensions in the top; cadence in D minor at 10.
#   10-11  Subject figures in both hands, toward B-flat and C minor.
#   12-13  Rising chromatic countersubject in the bass.
#   13-14  Subject, top, C minor.
#   14-15  Subject, bottom, C minor, the countersubject above.
#   16-17  Episode: suspensions over the running bass, back to G minor.
#   18-20  The opening returns: subject, top, G minor (from 18.5), over
#          the falling chromatic bass (19-20).
#   21-23  Coda; final cadence on a bare G (no Picardy third).

tempo 69

voice mid 0          # two voices only: there is no middle voice to set back

dyn 1 mf             # bars 1-9: exposition, G minor to D minor
lead 3-4 bottom      # subject, bass, G minor (bars 1-2 the top has it
                     # with the chromatic bass, an even balance is right)
lead 6-7 bottom      # subject, bass, D minor
broaden 9 0.94       # bar 9: hold back into the D minor cadence

dyn 10 mp            # bars 10-17: the darker middle, toward C minor,
                     # a softer manual
lead 12 bottom       # the chromatic countersubject rising in the bass
lead 13 top          # subject, top, C minor
lead 14-15 bottom    # subject, bass, C minor
broaden 17 0.95      # bar 17: a breath before the return

dyn 18 f             # bars 18-23: the opening returns in G minor
lead 19-20 top       # the subject's run in the top over the chromatic
                     # bass

rit 2 0.7            # bars 22-23 into the final G
